    {
      "id": "staggered",
      "group": "change",
      "title": "Staggered change",
      "question": "One chord change, spread across the voices?",
      "summary": "The bass move (A to B♭) comes first, then the upper voices follow (C♯–E to D–F). In this view, the momentary C♯–E over B♭ is mere 'coincident': C♯–E still 'belongs' to the A harmony.",
      "details": [
        "The colour groups describe a possible harmonic connection across time despite the fact that the relevant notes do not sound together.",
        "Compare with 'Neighbouring motion': the same notes can support a more local account of the B♭ arrival."
      ],
      "legend": [
        "A harmony / departing pitches",
        "B♭ harmony / arriving pitches",
        ""
      ],
