    {
      "id": "neighbour",
      "group": "change",
      "title": "Neighbouring motion",
      "question": "What if B♭ is already the harmony?",
      "summary": "Keep B♭ as the common tone and follow C♯→D and E→F. The two semitone approaches decorate the arrival of the B♭ triad.",
      "details": [
        "B♭–C♯–E can be read as an incomplete common-tone diminished seventh: C♯–E–[G]–B♭. G is implied, not written.",
        "This does not erase the connection with A: C♯ and E also belong to that harmony. The question is which relationship best explains this moment."
      ],
      "legend": [
