    {
      "id": "leading",
      "group": "return",
      "title": "Leading-note reading",
      "question": "Does G♯–D–F lead back to A?",
      "summary": "G♯ rises to A as D–F give way to C♯–E. This supports an incomplete leading-note seventh chord, with B♮ as its missing third.",
      "details": [
        "With a as a reference key: vii°7 → I, with a major tonic.",
        "If the key is d, then similarly vii°7/V → V.",
        "The absence of B♮ is significant: thirds are generally needed, especially if the B♮ would cancel the prevailing B♭.",
        "The A2 bass note between B♭ and G♯ is passing motion and also supports a passing 6/4 sonority (iv64 in d or i64 in a)."
      ],
      "legend": [
        "G♯–D–F: approach to A",
        "Return to A–C♯–E",
        "B♭–A: the preceding bass motion"
      ],
