    {
      "id": "sixth",
      "group": "return",
      "title": "Augmented-sixth reading",
      "question": "Might the earlier B♭ remain operative?",
      "summary": "An implied B♭ with D–F–G♯ gives the German augmented-sixth collection directed toward A. Here B♭ and G♯ occur successively in the bass.",
      "details": [
        "The B♭ ends before G♯ begins, they don't sound together;",
        "Neither, however is the B♭ cancelled by a B♮ which we might otherwise reasonably expect (cf the leading-note reading of G♯–D–F with no third).",
        "This view, Augmented-sixth (diminished third) reading would certainly have a bearing on key choice."
      ],
      "legend": [
        "Written G♯–D–F",
        "Return to A",
        "Earlier B♭, recalled by this reading"
      ],
