    {
      "id": "context",
      "group": "context",
      "title": "Cadential context",
      "question": "A clear cadence, an understated arrival",
      "summary": "The PAC in m.63 is clear but its rhetorical strength is understated beside the stronger dominant of mm.52–54, whose 'resolution' in m.55 begins with C alone before the (still not very conclusive A-minor in 6/3.",
      "details": [
        "Caplin is right to see m.63 as a perfect authentic cadence in the subordinate key.",
        "The issue explore here is the contrast in cadence emphasis, as part of the wider ambiguity at play here.",
        "A local arrival in a minor can coexist with a strong reminder of d in what follows.",
        "This ambiguity is another possible sleight of hand in a piece notorious for such effects."
      ],
      "legend": [
        "Dominant evidence and the approach to m.63",
        "Articulated tonic / cadential arrival",
        "m.55 begins on C alone"
      ],
